# Summary

This report estimates which parts of Singapore would be below, and hydraulically connected
to, extreme still-water sea levels today and under IPCC AR6 sea-level rise, and what lies
there. It is a screening exercise built entirely on open data; it is **not an official flood
map**. Coastal defences, drainage, waves and rainfall are not represented.

* The 100-year still-water level at Tanjong Pagar is {{rl100}} m above the 1995–2014 mean sea level
  (95 % CI {{rl100_lo}}–{{rl100_hi}} m), from a GEV fit to {{n_seasons}} detrended monsoon-season maxima.
* Adding AR6 medium-confidence projections gives 100-year levels of {{wl_100_2020}} m (2020) to
  {{wl_100_ssp585_2100}} m (SSP5-8.5, 2100).
* Connected flooding grows from {{area_100_2020}} km² to {{area_100_ssp585_2100}} km² (AR6 likely range
  {{area_100_ssp585_2100_lo}}–{{area_100_ssp585_2100_hi}} km²) across those cases; {{island_share}} of the 2100 SSP5-8.5 area is on
  offshore islands. Exposure is small: {{bldg_100_ssp585_2100}} buildings within ~60 m of
  flooding and a building-weighted proxy of {{pop_100_ssp585_2100}} residents.
* The dominant uncertainty is vertical: a ±0.3 m change in the geoid-to-MSL offset moves the
  2100 SSP5-8.5 area between {{dlo_100_ssp585_2100}} and {{dhi_100_ssp585_2100}} km²; ignoring the offset gives
  {{d0_100_ssp585_2100}} km². Hydraulic connectivity also matters: the naive bathtub gives {{naive_100_ssp585_2100}} km².
* A 2-D local-inertial model of one mainland sub-domain, verified against an analytical
  solution (RMSE {{hunter_rmse}} cm), floods {{m_ratio}} of the bathtub area under a realistic 72-hour
  storm-tide hydrograph, and {{m_steady_ratio}} when the peak is held constant.

# 1. Data

| Input | Source | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Hourly sea level | UHSLC station 699, Tanjong Pagar, 1984–2023 | {{n_hours}} valid hours, chart datum |
| Sea-level projections | IPCC AR6 (Garner et al. 2021), PSMSL 1746 | total RSL incl. VLM, medium confidence |
| Elevation | Copernicus GLO-30 DSM | 1″ (~{{cell_size}} m), EGM2008 heights |
| Geoid-to-MSL offset | DTU22MDT | {{mdt}} m mean over Singapore Strait cells |
| Boundaries, population | URA MP2019 planning areas and subzones; SingStat Census 2020 | data.gov.sg |
| Assets | OSM buildings ({{n_buildings}}) and roads ({{road_km_total}} km); LTA MRT exits ({{n_exits}}) | |

# 2. Methods

**Reference level.** All water levels are metres above the 1995–2014 mean sea level, which is
{{msl_cd}} m above the Tanjong Pagar chart datum and is also the AR6 baseline, so return levels and
projections add directly.

**Extremes.** The hourly record is detrended by the linear trend of annual means
({{trend}} ± {{trend_se}} mm/yr) anchored at 2005. Maxima are taken per July–June season
({{share_ne_monsoon}} fall in November–February); seasons with less than 80 % data are dropped. A GEV
is fitted by maximum likelihood (μ = {{gev_mu}}, σ = {{gev_sigma}}, ξ = {{gev_xi}}; bootstrap 95 % range of ξ
{{xi_lo}} to {{xi_hi}}; Gumbel not rejected, p = {{gumbel_p}}). Return-level intervals come from 1,000
non-parametric bootstrap resamples. Two cross-checks fall inside the interval: a GPD on
declustered exceedances of the 99.9th percentile ({{gpd_n}} clusters) gives {{rl100_gpd}} m for 100 years,
and removing each year's mean instead of a trend gives {{rl100_am}} m.

| Return period | Level (m) | 95 % CI (m) |
|---|---|---|
| 10 yr | {{rl10}} | {{rl10_lo}}–{{rl10_hi}} |
| 100 yr | {{rl100}} | {{rl100_lo}}–{{rl100_hi}} |
| 1,000 yr | {{rl1000}} | {{rl1000_lo}}–{{rl1000_hi}} |

![Return levels at Tanjong Pagar](../outputs/figures/return_levels.png){width=78%}

**Sea-level rise.** AR6 medians and likely ranges (17th–83rd percentiles) at Tanjong Pagar:

| Scenario | 2050 (m) | 2100 (m) |
|---|---|---|
| SSP1-2.6 | {{slr_ssp126_2050}} [{{slr_ssp126_2050_lo}}–{{slr_ssp126_2050_hi}}] | {{slr_ssp126_2100}} [{{slr_ssp126_2100_lo}}–{{slr_ssp126_2100_hi}}] |
| SSP2-4.5 | {{slr_ssp245_2050}} [{{slr_ssp245_2050_lo}}–{{slr_ssp245_2050_hi}}] | {{slr_ssp245_2100}} [{{slr_ssp245_2100_lo}}–{{slr_ssp245_2100_hi}}] |
| SSP5-8.5 | {{slr_ssp585_2050}} [{{slr_ssp585_2050_lo}}–{{slr_ssp585_2050_hi}}] | {{slr_ssp585_2100}} [{{slr_ssp585_2100_lo}}–{{slr_ssp585_2100_hi}}] |

The observed trend implies {{obs_rise_2020}} cm of rise from 2005 to 2020, consistent with the AR6
value of {{ar6_2020}} cm for 2020. The AR6 low-confidence high-end storyline is not included.

**Vertical datum.** Copernicus heights refer to the EGM2008 geoid, but local mean sea level
lies above the geoid by the mean dynamic topography. Heights are converted with
z_MSL = z_EGM2008 − {{mdt}} m (DTU22MDT). With the offset, {{below5_mdt}} of the land is below 5 m above MSL,
against {{below5_raw}} without it; the official figure is about 30 %, and a surface model should
under-count low ground, so the offset is directionally supported but not validated.

**Connected inundation.** Water cells in GLO-30 are flattened to 0 m. Zero cells linked to the
raster edge and outside the land polygons are open sea; zero cells inside the land polygons and
exactly flat patches of ≥ 300 cells are regulated inland water (barrage reservoirs), which
neither flood nor convey water. A priority flood gives each land cell the minimum still-water
level at which it connects to the sea; a scenario floods all cells whose fill level is below
its water level.

**2-D model.** The local-inertial equations (Bates et al. 2010) are solved on the 30 m grid of a
180 × 180-cell mainland sub-domain ({{m_box}}), forced at sea cells by the observed 1999-12-23
storm tide raised so its peak equals {{wl_100_ssp585_2100}} m. Verification: analytical front test
(RMSE {{hunter_rmse}} cm, {{hunter_rel}} of peak depth), lake-at-rest and mass-conservation tests (largest relative
mass error {{m_mass}}).

**Exposure.** Buildings and MRT exits count if a flooded cell is within ~60 m (the DSM puts
roof heights at the building itself); roads count where their own cell floods; residents are
Census 2020 subzone populations scaled by the share of exposed buildings.

